From: plastron
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: rk817: the level at a start, its current taken out

The level a start begins from is made from one sample the PMIC takes as it
powers on (PWRON_VOL), after 30 minutes or more off. That sample was read as
the battery's voltage at rest, though the current at that moment (PWRON_CUR)
moves it: a start from POWER samples while the SoC begins to draw, under the
rest, and a charger's start samples while the charger pushes current in,
above it. On a GKD Pixel 2 the OCV table is flat under 50% (3 to 4 mV a
point), so a few tens of mV are several points.

Now:

- The sample has its current's drop taken out (the device tree's internal
  resistance, 111 mOhm on a GKD Pixel 2) before the OCV table reads it.
- A charger's start (over 100 mA into the battery at the sample) keeps the
  coulomb counter, as a start after a short time off does. A level made from
  that sample was too high, charging counted up from it, and the next start
  from rest seemed to lose tens of points while off (a report on ZyperOS
  1.0.5: 85% when turned off, 49% two hours later).
- One line for each start: the level, where it came from, the time off, the
  start's source (ON_SOURCE), the sample, the voltage at rest and its level,
  and the counter's level. ZyperOS copies it into that start's own log.

A start after less than 30 minutes off, and a battery new to the gauge, are
as before, apart from the drop taken out of the new battery's sample.
---
 drivers/power/supply/rk817_charger.c | 175 ++++++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 136 insertions(+), 39 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/rk817_charger.c b/drivers/power/supply/rk817_charger.c
index 7838832..42b8eab 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/rk817_charger.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/rk817_charger.c
@@ -932,14 +932,93 @@ static int rk817_read_battery_nvram_values(struct rk817_charger *charger)
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * A start's level is made from the sample the PMIC takes as it powers on:
+ * the battery's voltage then (PWRON_VOL) and its current (PWRON_CUR, + into
+ * the battery, as BAT_CUR is). The OCV table gives the level of a battery at
+ * rest. One taking current stands above its rest by its internal
+ * resistance's drop, and one giving current stands under it, so the drop is
+ * taken out of the sample before the table reads it. Without a resistance in
+ * the device tree, the sample is read as it is.
+ *
+ * On a GKD Pixel 2 (111 mOhm in its device tree), 300 mA is 33 mV: about 4
+ * points at 60%, and 8 to 11 under 50%, where the table is flatter (3 to 4
+ * mV a point from 50 to 25%).
+ */
+static int rk817_rest_uv(int boot_uv, int boot_ua, int r_uohm)
+{
+	if (r_uohm <= 0)
+		return boot_uv;
+	return boot_uv - (int)div_s64((s64)boot_ua * r_uohm, 1000000);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Over this current into the battery, a start's sample is not used for the
+ * level, even with its drop taken out: this is a charger's start. The RK817
+ * powers a handheld on when a cable comes in, and it takes its sample while
+ * the charger already pushes current into the battery. That drop is large,
+ * and it is more than the device tree's resistance says (the board's path
+ * adds to the cell's own). A level made from such a sample is too high.
+ * Charging then counts up from the wrong level, and the next start from
+ * rest, after 30 minutes or more off, finds the truth. The level seems to
+ * fall by tens of points over the time off, with no charge lost (a report
+ * on ZyperOS 1.0.5: 85% when turned off, 49% two hours later). So a
+ * charger's start keeps the counter, as a start after a short time off
+ * does. A drift in the counter is set right at the next start from rest or
+ * at the next full charge.
+ *
+ * The rule is only for current into the battery. A start from POWER takes
+ * its sample while the SoC begins to draw current, and the resistance
+ * corrects that drop. A limit for that direction could stop every start
+ * from making the level again from the voltage. Besides a full charge, that
+ * is the only correction the counter gets.
+ */
+#define RK817_START_CHARGING_UA	100000
+
+/* Where a start's level comes from */
+enum rk817_start_from {
+	RK817_START_VOLTAGE,	/* off 30 min or more: the sample, at rest */
+	RK817_START_COUNTER,	/* off less: the counter, kept */
+	RK817_START_CHARGER,	/* a charger's start: the counter, kept */
+	RK817_START_NEW,	/* a battery new to the gauge: the sample */
+};
+
+/*
+ * off_min: the minutes OFF_CNT counted before this start (it counts tens of
+ * minutes, and stops at 2550). After 30 minutes or more off, the level is
+ * made again from the sample, as before: the driver's first author found
+ * that the counter drifts down when the RK817 is off for long. A battery
+ * new to the gauge has no counter to keep.
+ */
+static enum rk817_start_from rk817_start_from(bool new_battery, int off_min,
+					      int boot_ua)
+{
+	if (new_battery)
+		return RK817_START_NEW;
+	if (off_min < 30)
+		return RK817_START_COUNTER;
+	if (boot_ua > RK817_START_CHARGING_UA)
+		return RK817_START_CHARGER;
+	return RK817_START_VOLTAGE;
+}
+
 static int
 rk817_read_or_set_full_charge_on_boot(struct rk817_charger *charger,
 				struct power_supply_battery_info *bat_info)
 {
+	static const char *const from_text[] = {
+		[RK817_START_VOLTAGE] = "the voltage",
+		[RK817_START_COUNTER] = "the counter, off under 30 min",
+		[RK817_START_CHARGER] = "the counter, a charger's start",
+		[RK817_START_NEW] = "the voltage, a battery new to the gauge",
+	};
 	struct rk808 *rk808 = charger->rk808;
 	u8 bulk_reg[4];
 	u32 boot_voltage, boot_charge_mah;
-	int ret, reg, off_time, tmp;
+	int ret, reg, off_time = 0, tmp, on_source = 0;
+	int boot_ua, rest_uv, voltage_soc, counter_soc = -1;
+	enum rk817_start_from from;
+	char counter[16] = "none";
 	bool first_boot;
 
 	/*
@@ -950,27 +1029,38 @@ rk817_read_or_set_full_charge_on_boot(struct rk817_charger *charger,
 	if (ret < 0)
 		return ret;
 	first_boot = reg & RK817_BAT_CON;
+
 	/*
-	 * If the battery is uninitialized, use the poweron voltage and an ocv
-	 * lookup to guess our charge. The number won't be very accurate until
-	 * we hit either our minimum voltage (0%) or full charge (100%).
+	 * The sample the PMIC took as it powered on, with the drop of its
+	 * current taken out (rk817_rest_uv), and the level the OCV table gives
+	 * for it. Since only implementation has no working thermistor, assume
+	 * 20C for OCV lookup.
 	 */
+	regmap_bulk_read(rk808->regmap, RK817_GAS_GAUGE_PWRON_VOL_H,
+			 bulk_reg, 2);
+	tmp = get_unaligned_be16(bulk_reg);
+	boot_voltage = (charger->voltage_k * tmp) + 1000 * charger->voltage_b;
+	/* A signed 16 bit value, as BAT_CUR is */
+	regmap_bulk_read(rk808->regmap, RK817_GAS_GAUGE_PWRON_CUR_H,
+			 bulk_reg, 2);
+	tmp = (short int)get_unaligned_be16(bulk_reg);
+	boot_ua = ADC_TO_CURRENT(tmp, charger->res_div);
+	rest_uv = rk817_rest_uv(boot_voltage, boot_ua,
+				bat_info->factory_internal_resistance_uohm);
+	voltage_soc = power_supply_batinfo_ocv2cap(bat_info, rest_uv, 20) * 1000;
+	if (voltage_soc < 0)
+		voltage_soc = 0;
+	/* Only for the line below: POWER, a cable, a restart */
+	regmap_read(rk808->regmap, RK817_ON_SOURCE_REG, &on_source);
+
 	if (first_boot) {
-		regmap_bulk_read(rk808->regmap, RK817_GAS_GAUGE_PWRON_VOL_H,
-				 bulk_reg, 2);
-		tmp = get_unaligned_be16(bulk_reg);
-		boot_voltage = (charger->voltage_k * tmp) +
-				1000 * charger->voltage_b;
 		/*
-		 * Since only implementation has no working thermistor, assume
-		 * 20C for OCV lookup. If lookup fails, report error with OCV
-		 * table.
+		 * If the battery is uninitialized, use the poweron voltage and
+		 * an ocv lookup to guess our charge. The number won't be very
+		 * accurate until we hit either our minimum voltage (0%) or full
+		 * charge (100%).
 		 */
-		charger->soc = power_supply_batinfo_ocv2cap(bat_info,
-							    boot_voltage,
-							    20) * 1000;
-		if (charger->soc < 0)
-			charger->soc = 0;
+		charger->soc = voltage_soc;
 
 		/* Guess that full charge capacity is the design capacity */
 		charger->fcc_mah = charger->bat_charge_full_design_uah / 1000;
@@ -996,35 +1086,42 @@ rk817_read_or_set_full_charge_on_boot(struct rk817_charger *charger,
 			tmp = 0;
 		boot_charge_mah = ADC_TO_CHARGE_UAH(tmp,
 						    charger->res_div) / 1000;
+		counter_soc = boot_charge_mah * 1000 * 100 / charger->fcc_mah;
 		/*
 		 * Check if the columb counter has been off for more than 30
 		 * minutes as it tends to drift downward. If so, re-init soc
-		 * with the boot voltage instead. Note the unit values for the
-		 * OFF_CNT register appear to be in decaminutes and stops
-		 * counting at 2550 (0xFF) minutes. BSP kernel used OCV, but
-		 * for me occasionally that would show invalid values. Boot
-		 * voltage is only accurate for me on first poweron (not
-		 * reboots), but we shouldn't ever encounter an OFF_CNT more
-		 * than 0 on a reboot anyway.
+		 * with the boot voltage instead (rk817_start_from). Note the
+		 * unit values for the OFF_CNT register appear to be in
+		 * decaminutes and stops counting at 2550 (0xFF) minutes. BSP
+		 * kernel used OCV, but for me occasionally that would show
+		 * invalid values. Boot voltage is only accurate for me on first
+		 * poweron (not reboots), but we shouldn't ever encounter an
+		 * OFF_CNT more than 0 on a reboot anyway.
 		 */
 		regmap_read(rk808->regmap, RK817_GAS_GAUGE_OFF_CNT, &off_time);
-		if (off_time >= 3) {
-			regmap_bulk_read(rk808->regmap,
-					 RK817_GAS_GAUGE_PWRON_VOL_H,
-					 bulk_reg, 2);
-			tmp = get_unaligned_be16(bulk_reg);
-			boot_voltage = (charger->voltage_k * tmp) +
-					1000 * charger->voltage_b;
-			charger->soc =
-				power_supply_batinfo_ocv2cap(bat_info,
-							     boot_voltage,
-							     20) * 1000;
-		} else {
-			charger->soc = (boot_charge_mah * 1000 * 100 /
-					charger->fcc_mah);
-		}
 	}
 
+	from = rk817_start_from(first_boot, off_time * 10, boot_ua);
+	if (!first_boot)
+		charger->soc = from == RK817_START_VOLTAGE ? voltage_soc :
+							     counter_soc;
+
+	/*
+	 * One line for each start, with all that the level came from: ZyperOS
+	 * copies it into the start's own log, so a report of a level that
+	 * jumped shows why (the kernel's log is too small to keep it until
+	 * the logs are downloaded)
+	 */
+	if (counter_soc >= 0)
+		snprintf(counter, sizeof(counter), "%d%%",
+			 (counter_soc + 500) / 1000);
+	dev_info(charger->dev,
+		 "battery at start: %d%% from %s; off %d%s min, on 0x%02x, sampled %d mV at %d mA, at rest %d mV (%d%%), counter %s\n",
+		 (charger->soc + 500) / 1000, from_text[from], off_time * 10,
+		 off_time >= 0xff ? "+" : "", on_source,
+		 (int)boot_voltage / 1000, boot_ua / 1000, rest_uv / 1000,
+		 (voltage_soc + 500) / 1000, counter);
+
 	/*
 	 * Now we have our full charge capacity and soc, init the columb
 	 * counter.
